    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d122">

                <pb facs="0166" n="156" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg156"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek to President Roosevelt
                        </hi><anchor n="86" xml:id="d122-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-11-02">2 November 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I am in receipt of your telegrams transmitted to me by General Hearn on October
                        28th<anchor n="88" xml:id="d122-f2"/> and 29th<anchor n="88a"
                        xml:id="d122-f3"/> and November 1st.<anchor n="88b" xml:id="d122-f4"/> I am
                    delighted to accept the suggestions contained in your last telegram and am
                    looking forward to meeting you and Mr. Churchill. Everything <pb facs="0167"
                        n="157" xml:id="pg157"/> will be kept strictly secret here. The signing of
                    the Four Nation Declaration is a splendid success which is entirely due to your
                    firm stand for justice and solidarity. This declaration constitutes one of the
                    greatest contributions to the peace and security of the Post War World. Please
                    accept my warm and sincere thanks for your deep concern for our common cause and
                    kindly convey to Mr. Hull my appreciation of the excellent results he has
                    achieved at the conference. Best regards.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
